# "Freedom" appears as lawless freedom (S4) and as rational freedom guided by duty (S5, S6).
# Mill marks this as a change in how freedom is exercised, not in what freedom is: S5 says the reasonable "do not therefore desire freedom less."
# In S3, P2 and A1, freedom means not being subject to the will of others. That is the sense the conclusion's "liberated half" uses, so I found no bridge.
